  "notes_context": "These notes were distilled from earlier work in this repository that passed verification. Follow them unless the code or CHANGELOG.md shows they no longer hold.\n- [at-0004] Exclude bool when validating integer inputs: When validating that a value is a positive integer, check isinstance(x, bool) first because bool is a subclass of int; otherwise True would pass as quantity 1.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0001] Round money only with round_money: Always round monetary amounts with ledgerly.money.round_money (half-up to the cent); never use Python's round() or a bare Decimal.quantize(), which use banker's rounding and produce wrong invoice totals.\n  (learned from task E01 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0002] Use to_money and ZERO from ledgerly.money: Convert raw amounts with ledgerly.money.to_money and seed sums with ledgerly.money.ZERO, as invoice_total and line_total in ledgerly/invoice.py do, so Decimal arithmetic stays consistent.\n  (learned from task E01 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 1x, 1 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0003] Raise BillingError with stable snake_case codes: Reject invalid billing operations by raising BillingError(code, detail) from ledgerly.errors with a stable snake_case code such as \"invalid_state\", \"invalid_quantity\" or \"negative_amount\", and put the offending value in the detail via repr().\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)",
